.nh
.TH "SPLITX" "1" "Jan 2024" "gnux" "gnux manual"

.SH NAME
splitx - Split a file into pieces.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsplitx [OPTION]... [FILE [PREFIX]]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
splitx copies FILE (standard input when FILE is - or absent) into files named PREFIX (x by default) plus a suffix: xaa, xab, and so on, 1000 lines per file. -l N puts N lines in each file (the obsolete -N form too), -b SIZE puts SIZE bytes, and -C SIZE as many whole lines as fit in SIZE bytes, splitting only a line longer than that. SIZE takes the suffixes K, M, G, ... (powers of 1024) and KB, MB, ... (powers of 1000). -n CHUNKS makes a fixed number of files instead: N files of nearly equal size (the first ones a byte longer when the size does not divide), l/N without splitting lines (each line goes to the chunk it starts in), or r/N dealing lines out in turn; K/N, l/K/N and r/K/N write only the Kth chunk to standard output. To size the chunks of a pipe, splitx first copies it to a temporary file, as GNU does. Suffixes are two letters, and when they run out they grow (xyz is followed by xzaaa) so the names still sort in order; -a N fixes their length, -d and -x make them decimal or hexadecimal, --numeric-suffixes=FROM and --hex-suffixes=FROM start them at FROM, and --additional-suffix appends a fixed string such as .csv. -t SEP splits lines on SEP instead of newline ('\\0' for NUL), -e skips empty files in the -n modes, and --verbose reports each file as it is created. splitx refuses to overwrite FILE with one of its pieces. Only one way of splitting may be given. GNU's --filter, which pipes each piece through a shell command, is not implemented; -u is accepted and has no effect, since output is never held back. The digits of two obsolete -N options in a row run together (-5 -3 reads as -53).

.PP
EXIT STATUS.
  0  success: every operand was processed
  1  failure: an operand could not be processed (diagnosed on standard error), or a command-line mistake

.PP
HELP. splitx --help shows the options and the first examples; gnux help splitx prints the full manual, and gnux help splitx OPTION explains one option.

.PP
SEE ALSO. csplitx, catx, headx, tailx.

.PP
PERMISSIONS. Needs read permission on FILE, and write permission on the directory the pieces are created in (and on any existing piece, which is overwritten).

.PP
USE CASES.
  - Breaking a file too large for an email attachment or a FAT32 drive into pieces.
  - Dividing a big CSV or log into batches for parallel processing.
  - Spreading work over N workers with -n r/N or -n l/N.
  - Sampling one slice of a large file with -n K/N without writing the rest.
  - Reassembling the pieces later with catx, since the names sort in order.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--additional-suffix\fP=""
	append an additional \fBSUFFIX\fR to file names

.PP
\fB-b\fP, \fB--bytes\fP=""
	put \fBSIZE\fR bytes per output file

.PP
\fB-e\fP, \fB--elide-empty-files\fP[=false]
	do not generate empty output files with '-n'

.PP
\fB--help\fP[=false]
	display this help and exit

.PP
\fB-x\fP, \fB--hex-suffixes\fP[=]
	use hex suffixes starting at 0, not alphabetic; --hex-suffixes=FROM starts at FROM

.PP
\fB-C\fP, \fB--line-bytes\fP=""
	put at most \fBSIZE\fR bytes of records per output file

.PP
\fB-l\fP, \fB--lines\fP=""
	put \fBNUMBER\fR lines/records per output file

.PP
\fB-n\fP, \fB--number\fP=""
	generate \fBCHUNKS\fR output files: N, K/N, l/N, l/K/N, r/N or r/K/N

.PP
\fB-d\fP, \fB--numeric-suffixes\fP[=]
	use numeric suffixes starting at 0, not alphabetic; --numeric-suffixes=FROM starts at FROM

.PP
\fB-t\fP, \fB--separator\fP=[]
	use \fBSEP\fR instead of newline as the record separator; '\\0' (zero) specifies the NUL character

.PP
\fB-a\fP, \fB--suffix-length\fP=""
	generate suffixes of length \fBN\fR (default 2)

.PP
\fB-u\fP, \fB--unbuffered\fP[=false]
	immediately copy input to output with '-n r/...' (always the case here)

.PP
\fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	print a diagnostic just before each output file is opened

.PP
\fB--version\fP[=false]
	output version information and exit


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Cut a 10 GB backup into 2 GB pieces that fit on a FAT32 USB drive
  splitx -b 2G backup.tar.gz backup.tar.gz.part-

  # Put the pieces back together on the other machine
  catx backup.tar.gz.part-* > backup.tar.gz

  # Break a million-row CSV into files of 100,000 rows for an import tool that chokes on big files
  tailx -n +2 users.csv | splitx -l 100000 -d --additional-suffix=.csv - users-

  # Split a log into 4 roughly equal parts for 4 parallel workers, without cutting a line in half
  splitx -n l/4 access.log chunk-

  # Deal a list of URLs out to 3 crawlers in turn
  splitx -n r/3 urls.txt crawler-

  # Look at just the third quarter of a huge file without writing any pieces
  splitx -n l/3/4 huge.log | headx

  # Send a log in email-sized pieces of at most 5 MB, keeping lines whole
  splitx -C 5M app.log app.log.

  # Number the pieces from 1 instead of 0
  splitx -l 500 --numeric-suffixes=1 -a 3 list.txt list-

  # See each file as it is created
  splitx --verbose -l 2 names.txt

  # Split NUL-separated records, such as the output of find -print0
  find . -type f -print0 | splitx -t '\\0' -l 1000 - batch-

  # Split stdin into 1,000-line files the obsolete way, as old scripts do
  seqx 2500 | splitx -1000

  # Split and reassemble in the portable gnux shell, the same on every OS
  gnux shell -c 'splitx -b 1M video.mp4 part- && catx part-* > copy.mp4'
.EE
